I am performing Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) specifically for Audio Data. Unlike traditional tabular data (like spreadsheets), audio is a complex, time-dependent signal that requires transformation before we can truly understand its patterns.\n\nAudio EDA is the process of visualizing and statistically analyzing sound files to uncover features like pitch, rhythm, and noise levels. It helps us ensure the data is clean and balanced before we feed it into a machine learning model like Gemma.\n\n🎹 Key Components of Audio EDA\nTo \"see\" what our sound files look like, we typically focus on four primary visualizations:\n\n1. The Time Domain (Waveforms)\nThis is the most basic view. It shows the amplitude (loudness) of the sound over time.\n\nWhat it tells us: We can see silent gaps, clipping (when the sound is too loud and gets distorted), and the overall duration of the clips.\n\n2. The Frequency Domain (Spectrograms)\nSound is made of different frequencies (low bass, high treble). A spectrogram converts the waveform into a heat map.\n\nX-axis: Time\n\nY-axis: Frequency (Hz)\n\nColor: Intensity/Loudness\n\nWhat it tells us: It reveals \"hidden\" patterns, such as background hums or the specific harmonic structure of a voice.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import os\nimport random\nimport warnings\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport matplotlib.gridspec as gridspec\nimport matplotlib.ticker as ticker\nimport seaborn as sns\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nimport librosa\nimport librosa.display\n\nimport torch\nimport torchaudio\nimport torchaudio.transforms as AT\nimport torch.nn as nn\nfrom torch.utils.data import Dataset, DataLoader\nfrom torch.cuda.amp import autocast, GradScaler\nimport timm\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\nfrom tqdm.auto import tqdm\n\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\nprint(f\"PyTorch {torch.__version__} | torchaudio {torchaudio.__version__}\")\n